## Why This Is Asked
Fresh bit-hack that demonstrates mastery of fixed-step SWAR technique and portability across languages.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR fixed-masks and staged swaps
- Bitwise rotation-free reversal
- Unsigned 32-bit wrap handling in JavaScript/TypeScript

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverseBits32(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((n & 0x55555555) << 1);
  n = ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((n & 0x33333333) << 2);
  n = ((n >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  n = ((n >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  n = (n >>> 16) | (n << 16);
  return n >>> 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit integers in languages with 64-bit ops?
- How to verify correctness with quick property tests (e.g., n reversing twice yields n)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep bit-slicing, 128-bit composition, and Morton (Z-order) interleaving in a branchless, portable way.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order interleaving
- Bit-slicing with masks and shifts (part1by1 technique)
- 64-bit to 128-bit composition without branches
- Endianness implications for MSB-first construction

## Code Example
```c
struct uint128_t {
    uint64_t hi, lo;
};

// Spread single bits to double-bit positions
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x ^ (x << 16)) & 0x0000ffff0000ffff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 8))  & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 4))  & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
    x = (x ^ (x << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333;
    x = (x ^ (x << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555;
    return x;
}

struct uint128_t interleave64(uint64_t a, uint64_t b) {
    uint64_t a_spread = part1by1(a);
    uint64_t b_spread = part1by1(b);

    struct uint128_t result;
    result.lo = (a_spread << 1) | b_spread;
    result.hi = (a_spread >> 63) | (b_spread >> 63);
    return result;
}
```

This implementation uses the classic part1by1 technique to spread bits, then combines them with simple shifts and OR operations, achieving the required bit alternation without loops or conditionals.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type? (Explain any performance considerations.)
- Could you implement a generic macro to swap endianness for any multiple of 8 bytes?
